-- 243: EvoMentor US (preview), a small English space for evolution education researchers (2026-11-13).
--
-- What it is: the evolution thread of the US Next Generation Science Standards, 32 performance expectations from grade 1 to grade 12
-- (2 in K-2, 7 in 3-5, 12 in 6-8, 11 in 9-12): all LS4 expectations of grades 2 to 12, all heredity (LS3) expectations of grades 1 to 12, and a few from
-- ecosystems, Earth history and growth that bear on evolution. It mirrors what the German space EvoMentor Thuringia does for the Thuringia
-- curriculum, so researchers can look at a working example, compare the two and correct it.
--
-- Built from existing parts only, no new table and no new screen:
--   goals       one lpm_data_objects row per expectation (type performance_indicator). The text is the NGSS wording as read from nextgenscience.org
--               on 2026-11-13 through a page-summarizing fetch tool (not yet compared with the printed standards). content holds the foundation box (practice, core idea, crosscutting concept), the evolution
--               level with its reason, and the concept links, in the shape the Thuringia goals use (basiskonzeptbezug, strength 1 to 3).
--   concepts    lpm_schema_elements: the 7 NGSS crosscutting concepts as basic concepts (a hypothesis that they correspond to the six German
--               ones), and a root "Evolution concepts" with 52 concepts under it. That is an English draft of the German list in
--               EvoMentor_DE data/evolutionskonzepte.json, with the same ids, the same two levels and the German name kept in each concept.
--   links       63 concept links in content.basiskonzeptbezug (and the same links in lpm_object_tags). A link to a crosscutting concept comes
--               from the NGSS foundation box and has strength 3. A link to the evolution concepts carries our own level.
--   paths       2 threads for the Strands tab: variation, inheritance and selection (11 steps), and fossils, relatedness and deep time (8 steps).
--   rights      the NGSS source, its licence terms and the trademark footnote in project_source_declarations.
--
-- Evolution level: 6 goals name evolution explicitly (3), 21 cover the idea without the word (2), 5 are background only (1). The rule is
-- written into the definition of the root concept "Evolution concepts" and checked by the generator against the expectation text.
--
-- Review state: every goal, concept, link and path carries the agreed tag "LLM synthesized draft, awaiting review" (content.draftTag on goals and links,
-- metadata.review on concepts, the evidence note on paths). No expert has reviewed any of it. The status 'accepted' on goals only makes
-- Browse list them (see migration 235). It does not say a person reviewed them.
--
-- Space settings: private (visible to the owner and platform admins), maturity draft, English, no parent space. The owner is Dustin, found by
-- e-mail as in migrations 128 and 233. If that user is missing, the space is still created and an admin can add the owner later.
--
-- Rights: the expectation text belongs to NGSS Lead States and is used under the NGSS Public License. content_license stays empty (the ecosystem
-- default CC BY-NC-SA 4.0 for the lab's own tags and wording). The third-party terms sit in project_source_declarations, as RFC-0007 expects.
--
-- Idempotent: fixed uuid5 ids, ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING (or NOT EXISTS) on every insert. Running it twice changes nothing.
-- Generated by scripts/generate_evomentor_us_preview_migration.mjs from scripts/data/ngss-evolution-thread-us-2026-11-13.json. Do not hand-edit.
-- Rollback: DELETE FROM projects WHERE slug = 'evomentor-us-preview'; (everything below cascades).
SET search_path = public, extensions;

DO $MIGRATION$
DECLARE
  v_project UUID;
  v_branch UUID;
  v_user UUID;
BEGIN
  SELECT id INTO v_user FROM users WHERE email = 'dustin.eirdosh@eva.mpg.de';

  VALUES ('5a3ad6f8-1579-5910-b846-fa52c35ce857', 'evomentor-us-preview', $J$EvoMentor US (preview)$J$, $J$Standards are cultural models of what a society expects students to learn and how that learning grows over the years. This preview holds the evolution thread of the US Next Generation Science Standards (32 performance expectations from grade 1 to grade 12), each tagged for how much it has to do with evolution, so that evolution education researchers can look at it, compare it with the Thuringia space and correct our tags. We treat the seven NGSS crosscutting concepts as this space's basic concepts, on the hypothesis that they play the role of the six German basic concepts, and an expert needs to confirm that.$J$, 'active', 'in-development',
    $J$Preview for evolution education researchers. Each goal, concept, link and path carries its own review tag. The standards text is from nextgenscience.org (see Sources in Settings).$J$,
    'hosted', TRUE, 'draft', NULL, 'regional', ARRAY['US']::TEXT[], ARRAY['evolution','ngss','standards']::TEXT[], ARRAY['en']::TEXT[])
  ON CONFLICT (slug) DO NOTHING;
  SELECT id INTO v_project FROM projects WHERE slug = 'evomentor-us-preview';
  IF v_project IS NULL THEN RAISE EXCEPTION 'evomentor-us-preview missing'; END IF;
  IF v_user IS NOT NULL THEN
    UPDATE projects SET created_by = v_user WHERE id = v_project AND created_by IS NULL;
  END IF;
  SELECT id INTO v_branch FROM branches WHERE project_id = v_project AND is_trunk;
  IF v_branch IS NULL THEN RAISE EXCEPTION 'trunk branch of evomentor-us-preview missing'; END IF;

  -- Sources and rights
  SELECT v_project, $J$Next Generation Science Standards, performance expectations (NGSS Lead States, 2013), nextgenscience.org$J$, $J$Web pages (nextgenscience.org), statement wording copied from the page$J$, $J$NGSS Public License (https://www.nextgenscience.org/ngss-trademarks-and-copyright/ngss-trademarks-and-copyright): states, districts, schools, teachers and non-profit education entities may copy, reproduce, alter, adapt, edit, delete and rearrange any and all parts of the NGSS without permission. The licence page says that the National Academies Press holds the copyright of the published standards. Suggested citation: NGSS Lead States. 2013. Next Generation Science Standards: For States, By States. Washington, DC: The National Academies Press. The trademark guidance asks that a product using the name Next Generation Science Standards shows a footnote at its first prominent use: the name is a registered trademark of WestEd, and neither WestEd nor the lead states and partners were involved in the production of the product or endorse it. The guidance also asks that the name is used in full. Open questions: the licence page does not define a non-profit education entity and does not mention research use. Written clarification from WestEd has not been requested yet. These terms were read through a page-summarizing fetch tool on 2026-11-13. Read the licence page itself before the space is shared outside the lab. This space copies the text of the expectations and the names of the foundation box items, and nothing else from NGSS. Everything else in this space (tags, levels, English concept names, paths) is the lab's own draft.$J$, 'full-text-stored', $J$https://www.nextgenscience.org/$J$
  WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM project_source_declarations WHERE project_id = v_project AND source_name = $J$Next Generation Science Standards, performance expectations (NGSS Lead States, 2013), nextgenscience.org$J$);
  SELECT v_project, $J$Evolution concept list of EvoMentor Thuringia (German), with English names drafted for this space$J$, $J$JSON list of 52 concepts, two levels$J$, $J$Own work of the lab. The German original is in the repository EvoMentor_DE (data/evolutionskonzepte.json), and the Thuringia space uses it. The English names and definitions in this space are drafts translated from it. LLM synthesized draft, awaiting review. Shared under the ecosystem default licence (CC BY-NC-SA 4.0).$J$, 'full-text-stored', NULL
  WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM project_source_declarations WHERE project_id = v_project AND source_name = $J$Evolution concept list of EvoMentor Thuringia (German), with English names drafted for this space$J$);

  -- Concepts: the root concepts first, then the two levels of evolution concepts (each statement only points at rows of an earlier one)
  ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

  ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

  ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

  -- Goals: one per expectation, on the trunk branch with status accepted so that Browse lists them
  ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

  -- The same concept links in the table the Coherence screen reads
  ON CONFLICT (data_object_id, schema_element_id) DO NOTHING;

  -- Paths for the Strands tab
  VALUES ('df083d88-57e4-5e9d-a59a-f34e55bb2b33', v_project, v_branch, 'ngss-variation-inheritance-selection', $J$Variation, inheritance and selection from grade 1 to grade 12$J$, 'vertical', '2b5c4785-a48c-5103-8a64-81747f862ab9', $J$How does the idea that offspring differ a little from their parents grow into an explanation of natural selection?$J$, $J$The path starts in grade 1 with young plants and animals that look like their parents but differ in small ways. In grade 3 students link variation to survival and reproduction. In middle school they add genes, mutation and sexual reproduction as sources of variation, then describe natural selection with numbers. In high school they name the factors behind evolution and explain adaptation. The order follows the core idea codes LS3.A, LS3.B, LS4.B and LS4.C that NGSS gives each expectation.$J$, $J$At which grade does the path first need the idea that some individuals leave more offspring than others? Does 3-LS4-2 already carry it?$J$, $J$LLM synthesized draft, awaiting review. Built from the core idea codes in the foundation boxes of the listed expectations. NGSS publishes its own progressions of core ideas. This draft has not been compared with them.$J$, $J$["None of the grade K-5 expectations in this space uses the word evolution or natural selection.","The idea that populations change over generations is first stated in middle school (MS-LS4-6)."]$J$::jsonb, 'proposed')
  ON CONFLICT (project_id, slug) DO NOTHING;
  ON CONFLICT (thread_id, sequence) DO NOTHING;

  VALUES ('3d64f5d3-51a3-5ce2-8012-f0de97abb601', v_project, v_branch, 'ngss-fossils-relatedness-deep-time', $J$Fossils, relatedness and deep time from grade 3 to grade 12$J$, 'vertical', '852cb59b-7135-5056-a3e7-cbf9189d8874', $J$How do students come to see the fossil record and body similarities as evidence that living things share ancestors?$J$, $J$In grade 3 fossils tell students about organisms and places of long ago. In middle school students place events on the geologic time scale, read patterns of change in the fossil record and compare bodies and embryos across species to infer relationships. In high school they pull the lines of evidence together, weigh how environmental change can lead to new species and extinctions, and look at how Earth's systems and life changed together. The order follows the core idea codes LS4.A, LS4.C and ESS1.C that NGSS gives each expectation.$J$, $J$Which expectation first lets students say that two species share an ancestor? Is the step from fossils to relatedness made explicit anywhere before middle school?$J$, $J$LLM synthesized draft, awaiting review. Built from the core idea codes in the foundation boxes of the listed expectations. NGSS publishes its own progressions of core ideas. This draft has not been compared with them.$J$, $J$["No expectation in this space asks students to read or build a family tree of species.","Deep time appears only as the geologic time scale in MS-ESS1-4."]$J$::jsonb, 'proposed')
  ON CONFLICT (project_id, slug) DO NOTHING;
  ON CONFLICT (thread_id, sequence) DO NOTHING;

END $MIGRATION$;
